Optimisation objectives:
The water-quality sensor-placement problem has three principal objectives.
1. Minimise expected contamination detection time:
\[
f_1=\min\left\{\frac{1}{N_s}
\sum_{s=1}^{N_s}t_{d}^{s}(X)\right\}
\]
where \(N_s\) is the number of contamination events and \(t_d^s(X)\) is the detection time for event \(s\) using sensor configuration \(X\).
2. Minimise expected contaminated-water volume:
\[
f_2=\min\left\{\frac{1}{N_s}
\sum_{s=1}^{N_s}\sum_{i=1}^{N}
\sum_{t=t_s^{in}}^{t_d^s}V_i^s(t)\right\}
\]
where \(V_i^s(t)\) represents the contaminated water volume at junction \(i\) during event \(s\).
3. Maximise detection likelihood:
\[
f_3=\max\left\{\frac{1}{N_s}
\sum_{s=1}^{N_s}d_s(X)\right\}
\]
where \(d_s=1\) if event \(s\) is detected and \(0\) otherwise.

The broader framework also considers hydraulic, energy and topological performance.
